          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Find the dual polyhedron to each of the Platonic solids.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Tetrahedron: tetrahedron (self-dual).\n\nCube: octahedron.\n\nOctahedron: cube.\n\nDodecahedron: icosahedron.\n\nIcosahedron: dodecahedron.",
      "worked": "Place a vertex of the dual at the centre of each face and join vertices whose original faces share an edge.\n\nThe tetrahedron has four triangular faces, with three faces meeting at each vertex, so its dual is another tetrahedron.\n\nThe cube has six faces, with three meeting at each vertex. Its dual has six vertices and eight triangular faces: an octahedron. Reversing this correspondence gives the cube as the dual of the octahedron.\n\nThe dodecahedron has twelve faces, with three meeting at each vertex. Its dual has twelve vertices and twenty triangular faces: an icosahedron. Reversing this correspondence gives the dodecahedron as the dual of the icosahedron.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
